@Tutorial(time: 25) {
    @Intro(title: "Dependency Injection (Reducer)") {
        This is the Reducer-pattern counterpart to <doc:01-04-Injection>: same goal — externalize system dependencies (dates, persistence, network) so business logic stays testable — expressed with `Injectable`, `ReducerDependencies`, and `dependencies.resolve()` inside a static `update()` instead of `@Injected` properties on a class.
    }

    @Section(title: "Designing dependencies") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Dependencies come in two shapes. Most of the time a dependency has exactly one real
            behavior, so a struct conforming to `Injectable` with a `defaultValue` is enough — that's
            `DateProvider` and `PersistenceProvider` below. When a dependency genuinely has more than
            one plausible implementation (a real network call vs. a canned response in tests), reach
            for a real Swift protocol instead — that's `NetworkProvider`. Either way, the difference
            starts and ends at declaration: they're *used* identically, inside a Reducer's `update()`.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Start with a date provider. Instead of calling `Date()` directly inside business logic, inject a `DateProvider` so tests can supply a fixed date.

                @Code(name: "Dependencies.swift", file: Injection-Reducer-DateProvider-01.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                Next, a persistence provider abstracting `UserDefaults` — reading and writing a list of favorited articles.

                @Code(name: "Dependencies.swift", file: Injection-Reducer-PersistenceProvider-01.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                Finally, a network provider for fetching articles. Unlike the two dependencies above, this one is declared as a genuine `protocol` with a `RealNetworkProvider` conformance, not an `Injectable` struct-of-closures — the next two sections show why that choice doesn't cost anything at the call site. `RealNetworkProvider` also conforms to `Injectable` itself, declaring its default with a protocol return type (`static var defaultValue: NetworkProvider`) instead of `Self` — needed later, once `@ReducerInjected` wants to read `NetworkProvider` declaratively (see "Reducer-only: @ReducerInjected" below): a protocol can't supply its own `Self`-typed default the way a concrete type can, so a named concrete type has to.

                @Code(name: "Dependencies.swift", file: Injection-Reducer-NetworkProvider-01.swift)
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Resolving dependencies in update()") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A Reducer's `update()` receives a `dependencies: ReducerDependencies` parameter. Call `try dependencies.resolve()` wherever you need a dependency — there's no property wrapper required for this style, resolution happens imperatively, right where it's used. Nothing about this call site changes whether the resolved type is an `Injectable` struct or a protocol — `let network: NetworkProvider = try dependencies.resolve()` reads identically either way.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                `NewsFeedListReducer` resolves all three dependencies across its event handlers: `PersistenceProvider` and `NetworkProvider` on load, `DateProvider` and `PersistenceProvider` when toggling a favorite. Note the network fetch becomes an effect — `effectsState.enqueue(...)` — since it's asynchronous, while persistence and date reads happen synchronously inline.

                @Code(name: "NewsFeedListReducer.swift", file: Injection-Reducer-Reducer-01.swift)
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Wiring up the real dependencies") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Before looking at tests, it's worth seeing where injection happens for the *real* app — it's easy to come away thinking `.injectObject(...)` is only a testing device. Unlike `@Injected`/`@ReducerInjected`, `dependencies.resolve()` never falls back to a declared default on a miss — it just throws, regardless of whether the type is a concrete struct or a protocol. So all three dependencies need a real value injected once here, even though `@ReducerInjected` (further below) would happily use their declared defaults automatically.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Wherever your app creates this Store for real use — a SwiftUI `@StateObject`, your composition root, wherever that is for your app — inject all three dependencies once. `DateProvider.defaultValue`, `PersistenceProvider.defaultValue`, and `RealNetworkProvider.defaultValue` all come from the same `Injectable` conformance — the only difference is which type `defaultValue` returns.

                @Code(name: "AppSetup.swift", file: Injection-Reducer-RealSetup-01.swift)
            }

            From here on, this Store behaves exactly like the one in every earlier section — `update()` doesn't know or care whether a dependency was injected for real use or for a test.
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Testing with injected fakes") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Tests inject fakes the same way as classic Statostore — `.injectObject(...)` on the store before running the test plan — the fixtures just get resolved differently on the other end (`dependencies.resolve()` instead of `@Injected`). Swapping a value-type dependency (`DateProvider`, `PersistenceProvider`) means reconstructing the *same* struct with different closures; swapping a protocol-typed one (`NetworkProvider`) means providing a *different* conforming type — here, `FakeNetworkProvider`.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Inject a fixed `DateProvider`, an in-memory `PersistenceProvider`, and a `FakeNetworkProvider` returning canned articles, then drive the flow with `GIVEN`/`WHEN`/`THEN` — including completing the network effect with `WHEN_OlderEffectCompletes`. `FakeNetworkProvider` is injected via `injectObject(_:for: RealNetworkProvider.self)` rather than a bare `injectObject(_:)` — pinning the registration to `NetworkProvider` (`RealNetworkProvider`'s own `InjectedValue`), so there's no upcast to remember and no way to get it wrong.

                @Code(name: "InjectionTests.swift", file: Injection-Reducer-Test-01.swift)
            }

            With dependencies injected, the test controls the date, the "saved" favorites, and the network response — no real clock, disk, or network involved, and the assertions read as acceptance criteria for the feature.
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Reducer-only: @ReducerInjected") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The Reducer pattern also offers a **declarative** injection style with no classic-Statostore equivalent: `@ReducerInjected`, a property wrapper placed directly on a `State` struct field. Instead of calling `dependencies.resolve()` inside every handler that needs a dependency, declare it once and read it anywhere in `State` — including from outside `update()`, since it resolves into the Store's computed `state` getter. It reuses the exact same `DateProvider`/`NetworkProvider` from earlier — `@ReducerInjected` works identically for both, since both are just `Injectable` conformances that happen to return a different type from `defaultValue`.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                `NewsFeedStatusReducer` declares both dependencies once on `State` — `@ReducerInjected(DateProvider.self)` and `@ReducerInjected(RealNetworkProvider.self)` — then reads `state.date`/`state.network` directly inside `update()`. Compare with the `dependencies.resolve()` call sites in `NewsFeedListReducer` above: the declaration only happens once here, and `state.network`'s type annotation is `NetworkProvider` (the protocol) even though the wrapper is handed `RealNetworkProvider.self` (the concrete type that owns the default) — exactly the same shape as `@Injected` on a classic `Statostore`.

                @Code(name: "NewsFeedStatusReducer.swift", file: Injection-Reducer-ReducerInjected-01.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                With nothing injected, `DateProvider.defaultValue` and `RealNetworkProvider.defaultValue` are used automatically — no crash, no special-casing needed in tests that don't care about these dependencies. Unlike `dependencies.resolve()`, `@ReducerInjected` never throws, for either dependency shape.

                @Code(name: "NewsFeedStatusReducerTests.swift", file: Injection-Reducer-DefaultTest-01.swift)
            }
            @Step {
                Injecting real values for both — a fixed `DateProvider` and a `FakeNetworkProvider` via `injectObject(_:for:)` — swaps what `state.date`/`state.network` resolve to, exactly like the imperative style above.

                @Code(name: "NewsFeedStatusReducerTests.swift", file: Injection-Reducer-InjectedTest-01.swift)
            }

            Reach for `@ReducerInjected` when a dependency is used in several places across a reducer (or needs to be read outside `update()`); reach for `dependencies.resolve()` when it's needed in exactly one handler and you'd rather see it resolved right at the call site. The choice is about *where* you want to read a dependency, not what kind of dependency it is — both `Injectable` and protocol-typed dependencies work identically either way.
        }
    }
}
